second artist, birth date for a different person, or second sport is evidence to retain it. Check each proposed deletion once more against all originals before producing the final short list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"When did the Women's Strike take place?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 50, \"text\": \"the word about the Day Off throughout the country.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"start\": 51, \"end\": 202, \"text\": \"The Day Off event organizers got radio stations, television, and newspapers to run stories about gender-based discrimination and lower wages for women.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"start\": 203, \"end\": 246, \"text\": \"The event garnered international attention.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"start\": 247, \"end\": 368, \"text\": \"On 24 October 1975, Icelandic women did not go to their paid jobs nor did they do any housework or child-rearing at home.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"start\": 369, \"end\": 441, \"text\": \"Ninety percent of women took part, including women in rural communities.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"start\": 442, \"end\": 516, \"text\": \"Fish factories were closed since the factory workers were primarily women.\"}, \"D1S7\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"start\": 517, \"end\": 618, \"text\": \"During the Day Off, 25,000 out of a population of 220,000 people in Iceland gathered in the center of\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 122, \"text\": \"the position for 16 years - years that set Iceland on course to become known as `` the world 's most feminist country '' .\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d01\", \"start\": 123, \"end\": 340, \"text\": \"But Vigdis insists she would never have been president had it not been for the events of one sunny day - 24 October 1975 - when 90 % of women in the country decided to demonstrate their importance by going on strike .\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d01\", \"start\": 341, \"end\": 518, \"text\": \"Image source , Women 's History Archives Instead of going to the office , doing housework or childcare they took to the streets in their thousands to rally for equal rights with\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 18, \"text\": \"disambiguation ) .\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d02\", \"start\": 19, \"end\": 314, \"text\": \"1975 Icelandic women 's strike Women 's Day Off Date 24 October 1975 Location Iceland Caused by Gender pay gap , occupational sexism Goals To `` demonstrate the indispensable work of women for Iceland 's economy and society '' and `` protest wage discrepancy and unfair employment practices '' .\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d02\", \"start\": 315, \"end\": 568, \"text\": \"Methods Women 's strike On 24 October 1975 , Icelandic women went on strike for the day to `` demonstrate the indispensable work of women for Iceland 's economy and society '' [ 1 ] and to `` protest wage discrepancy and unfair employment practices '' .\"}, \"D4S1\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d03\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 185, \"text\": \"2018 Spanish women's strike On March 8, 2018, International Women's Day, Spanish women went on strike for the day to denounce sexual discrimination, domestic violence and the wage gap .\"}, \"D4S2\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d03\", \"start\": 186, \"end\": 430, \"text\": \"Participants, led by women's organizations and the trade unions, did not go to their paid jobs, especially in education, and did not do any housework or child-rearing for the whole day; some groups additionally called for a consumption strike .\"}, \"D4S3\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d03\", \"start\": 431, \"end\": 585, \"text\": \"The unions estimate that 5 million women participated in the strike, with massive demonstrations taking place in the most populated cities of the country.\"}, \"D4S4\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d03\", \"start\": 586, \"end\": 612, \"text\": \"The action was part of the\"}, \"D5S1\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d04\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 204, \"text\": \"War, about 50,000 women brought together by Women Strike for Peace marched in 60 cities in the United States to demonstrate against nuclear weapons under the slogan \\\"End the Arms Race not the Human Race\\\".\"}, \"D5S2\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d04\", \"start\": 205, \"end\": 275, \"text\": \"It was the largest national women's peace protest of the 20th century.\"}, \"D5S3\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d04\", \"start\": 276, \"end\": 386, \"text\": \"About 1,500 women led by Dagmar Wilson gathered at the foot of the Washington Monument while President John F.\"}, \"D5S4\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d04\", \"start\": 387, \"end\": 436, \"text\": \"Kennedy watched from a window at the White House.\"}, \"D5S5\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d04\", \"start\": 437, \"end\": 557, \"text\": \"The protest helped \\\"push the United States and the Soviet Union into signing a nuclear test-ban treaty two years later\\\".\"}, \"D5S6\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d04\", \"start\": 558, \"end\": 589, \"text\": \"In January 1962, Berkeley Women\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Women's Strike\", \"scope\": \"Iceland\", \"claim\": \"On 24 October 1975, Icelandic women did not go to their paid jobs nor did they do any housework or child-rearing at home.\", \"answer\": \"24 October 1975\", \"quote\": \"On 24 October 1975, Icelandic women did not go to their paid jobs nor did they do any housework or child-rearing at home.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Women's Strike\", \"scope\": \"Iceland\", \"claim\": \"The Women's Strike in Iceland took place on 24 October 1975.\", \"answer\": \"24 October 1975\", \"quote\": \"But Vigdis insists she would never have been president had it not been for the events of one sunny day - 24 October 1975 - when 90 % of women in the country decided to demonstrate their importance by going on strike .\", \"original_document_number\": 2}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d02\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d02:c0\", \"entity\": \"Women's Strike\", \"scope\": \"Icelandic women's strike\", \"claim\": \"The Icelandic women's strike took place on 24 October 1975.\", \"answer\": \"24 October 1975\", \"quote\": \"1975 Icelandic women 's strike Women 's Day Off Date 24 October 1975 Location Iceland Caused by Gender pay gap , occupational sexism Goals To `` demonstrate the indispensable work of women for Iceland 's economy and society '' and `` protest wage discrepancy and unfair employment practices '' . Methods Women 's strike On 24 October 1975 , Icelandic women went on strike for the day to `` demonstrate the indispensable work of women for Iceland 's economy and society '' [ 1 ] and to `` 